NAME
    nohup - run a command immune to hangups

SYNOPSIS
    nohup COMMAND [ARG...]

DESCRIPTION
    Runs COMMAND with SIGHUP set to be ignored, so it keeps running
    when the terminal that started it goes away instead of being
    killed with the session. Because that output would otherwise be
    lost with the terminal, the command's standard output - when it
    is a terminal - is appended to nohup.out in the current
    directory, created with mode 0600 if it does not exist, and its
    standard error is sent to the same place; standard input, when
    it is a terminal, is redirected from /dev/null. A short note on
    standard error says what was redirected.

    nohup then replaces itself with the command (execve(2)), so the
    shell sees the command's own exit status. A name without a slash
    is looked up in /bin; there is no PATH search and no
    $HOME/nohup.out fallback.

EXIT STATUS
    The command's status. nohup returns 125 if it could not set up
    (bad usage, nohup.out not creatable) and 127 if the command
    could not be executed at all.

SEE ALSO
    coreutils(1), sh(1), man(1)
